T|                                                            OVER BLACK:             
N|               We hear --                                                           
O|
C,E|                                   ETHELRIDA (V.O.)                                 
U|                         My history report, by Ethelrida                            
U|                         Pearl Smutny.                                              
O|
N|               MUSIC UP                                                             
O|
T|                                                            HARD IN ON:             
O|
N|               A CINDER BLOCK WALL                                                  
N|               White, non-descript.                                                 
O|
N|               We are in --                                                         
O|
S|       1       INT. PRINCIPAL’S OFFICE. GROVER CLEVELAND HIGH SCHOOL. KANSAS  1     
S|               CITY, MO - DAY (1950)                                                
O|
N,I,G|               A young BIRACIAL GIRL (16) enters frame, as if pushed. This          
N,I|               is ETHELRIDA PEARL SMUTNY. Her parents (white father, black          
N|               mother) own a mortuary, and so she always smells vaguely of          
N|               formaldehyde. Which, as you would imagine, is not ideal for a        
N|               teenage girl.                                                        
N|               It is 1950.                                                          
O|
C,E|                                   ETHELRIDA (V.O.)                                 
U|                         Frederick Douglass, runaway slave,                         
U|                         once intoned -- I stand before you                         
U|                         as a thief and a robber. I stole                           
U|                         this head, these limbs, this body                          
U|                         from my master, and ran off with                           
U|                         them.                                                      
O|
G,I|               REVERSE ON MS. HAGBLOOM                                              
N,I|               The Principal’s secretary. A heavyset humorless Polish-              
N,I|               American woman. She stares at Ethelrida with disdain.                
O|
C|                                   MS. HAGBLOOM                                     
U|                         What’d you do this time?                                   
O|
G|               ANGLE ON ETHELRIDA                                                   
O|
N|               It’s clear she gets dragged to the principal’s office a lot.         
O|
C|                                   ETHELRIDA                                        
U|                         I been maligned.                                           
G|               BEHIND HAGBLOOM                                                      
O|
O|            No portion of this script may be performed, published, reproduced, sold or distributed by any means or quoted or published
O|       in any medium, without prior written consent of FX Productions, LLC. In no event, may any participant monetize or profit off the script in any way.
O|                              © 2018 FX Productions, LLC. All rights reserved.      
O|            F A R G O #401 Blue Rev. - 11/18/19 2.     
O|
N,G|               we can see PRINCIPAL RICE CRISCOE through the window of his          
N|               office. He’s WHACKING another student’s behind with a large          
N|               paddle.                                                              
N|               Criscoe is a ruddy Irishman with hams for hands. This is how         
N|               it works in the Land of the Free and the Home of the Brave.          
N|               All of us are from somewhere else originally.                        
O|
C,E|                                   ETHELRIDA (V.O.)                                 
U|                         My point being, the moment our feet                        
U|                         touched American soil we were                              
U|                         already criminals.                                         
O|
G|               ANGLE ON PRINCIPAL CRISCOE                                           
N|               as he comes to his office door, holding the paddle. He’s             
N|               sweating, out of breath. He brushes his comb over into place         
N|               and glowers at Ethelrida, while the beaten student shuffles          
N|               out, crying.                                                         
O|
C|                                   PRINCIPAL CRISCOE                                
U|                         Next.                                                      
O|
T|                                                                CUT TO:             
O|
S|       2       INT. HALLWAY. GROVER CLEVELAND HIGH SCHOOL. KANSAS CITY, MO -  2     
S|               DAY (1950)                                                           
N|               Ms. HAGBLOOM walks ETHELRIDA down the hall. She walks                
N|               gingerly, feeling the burn of her discipline.                        
O|
C,E|                                   ETHELRIDA (V.O.)                                 
U|                         But this report isn’t about my                             
U|                         history. It’s about our history.                           
O|
T|                                                                CUT TO:             
O|
S|       3       EXT. JACKSON DEMOCRATIC CLUB. KANSAS CITY, MO - DAY (1900)     3     
N|               The Year 1900 --                                                     
O|
N|               The street is bustling with activity, merchants with carts           
N,G|               selling wares. Horse-drawn carriages. The people we see are          
N|               mostly Jewish.                                                       
O|
C,E|                                   ETHELRIDA (V.O.)                                 
U|                         Kansas City, Missouri. In the                              
U|                         beginning there were the Hebrews,                          
U|                         and they ran the underworld.                               
O|
